WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
CARTER MORGAN: We got
rid of over 600 bugs

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.520
in the last video.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.930
So the Add a Cat to That
app should be pretty secure,

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.268
except I can't
remember if I removed

00:00:09.268 --> 00:00:11.310
the debug switch in the
code I checked in at 2:00

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.440
AM last night.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.180
Stick around to see
how Web Security

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.140
Scanner helps protect you from
inevitable mistakes like this.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.730
Mistakes happen even
to the best of us.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.600
No matter how great
developers are,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.590
we're going to
accidentally introduce bugs

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:26.910
to the code base.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.980
Luckily, Google's
Web Security Scanner

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.640
can do automatic testing to
your deployed code to find bugs

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.100
and unintentional
vulnerabilities introduced

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.880
to the code base.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.410
It's really easy to use.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.110
Let's see it in action.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.650
From this simple web UI in
GCP, we create a new scan.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.760
We point it at a
staging URL for our app,

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.220
not the production URL.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.600
Because Cloud
Security Scanner is

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.880
going to be fuzz testing,
or throwing a whole lot

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.530
of wacky data, at our app.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.030
And we don't want
to risk corrupting

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.860
the live version of our app.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.750
In this example, we've put in
a Kubernetes pod address there.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.500
I'll leave everything
else that is default

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.410
and then set Cloud Web
Security Scanner to run daily.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.240
And after about 15
minutes, you should

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.670
be able to see what
the output looks like.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.730
And we see here, Cloud
Web Security Scanner

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.410
found a few issues.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.990
See, Cloud Web Security
Scanner starts crawling around

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.900
all the URLs it can find and
starts looking for these known

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.190
vulnerabilities.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:23.040
Here you see it found some
cross site scripting issues.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.720
By clicking on the
known vulnerability,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.480
we can see the issue in
our admin login page.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.120
Just from the UI, we're able
to dig in and get the exact way

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.450
to replicate that bug so that
we can troubleshoot and then fix

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.140
it.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.033
So we're going to show
you this bug live.

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:38.700
And then we're going
to fix it right now

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.860
so that we can knock yet
another vulnerability off

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.110
of Add a Cat to That.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.750
If we take that HTML from
Web Security Scanner,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.580
we can see right in
our UI that instead

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.370
of showing the email address of
the person who tried to log in,

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.190
our app is displaying
weird stuff.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.680
If we go and look at
the login page HTML,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.530
where Web Security Scanner
got that HTML from,

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.140
we can look and see
that I accidentally

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.770
left in a safe flag which
disables cross site scripting

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:05.950
prevention.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.669
Once we take that out and
push that build to production,

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:11.260
Web Security Scanner will
continue doing its daily scan

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.510
and letting us know about
any other vulnerabilities it

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.940
can detect.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.000
The coolest part about this
whole process, and the tool,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.850
is that Web Security
Scanner doesn't cost you

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.110
anything to use.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.510
It's a free and easy low
friction fire and forget tool.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:26.400
It's not perfect.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.770
It won't protect you from
a sophisticated attack

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.480
or a new clever zero day.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.760
But it will help you with
a lot of those common,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.730
easy to prevent errors that
may have slipped past your code

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.110
review process.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:41.130
Once you set up that daily
scan, it's going to keep going.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.970
The supply chain is secure.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.120
We've set up automatic
web security scans.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.520
This app should be secure.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.410
What's left?

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:49.970
Me.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:50.470
Me.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.050
Oh, me.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:51.870
Phishing.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:52.560
That's right.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:53.870
We've protected our software.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.790
It's time to protect
our developers.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:57.230
Don't miss the next video.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:58.730
We're going to talk
about preventing

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.860
one of the oldest and most
common vulnerabilities,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.410
phishing.

